{-
   Why the take-out-and-put-back rule is narrower than "an exclusive loan".

   `let &d = c` re-lends storage this frame is *also* tracking, under `c`'s own move path. A take
   through the alias would empty one place while the other still said it held a value - two answers
   to one question, which is the arrangement this stage exists to not have - and the hole would then
   be observable from `c` between the two, where a `&` parameter's lender is the caller and holds the
   argument exclusively for the length of the call.

   Refused here rather than in `Reject.BorrowRefill.yana` because resolution stops before the
   analysis runs, and a fixture holding both would only ever report one of them.

   The fix is the one the language already has: `->c` names the value the author meant to move.
-}

fn eat(->s: String) -> Int = truncate(length(s)) :: Int

fn aliased(->owned: String) -> Int:
    let &d = owned
    let n = eat(d)
    d = "put back"
    return n

fn main() -> Int = 0
